"A bounded context map shows where domain models apply and how separately owned models integrate or translate.\n\nUse when: Resolve conflicting meanings of shared domain words. Plan integration responsibilities between teams and services.\n\nAvoid when: The objective is a detailed database schema or network route.\n\nVisual grammar: Nodes represent bounded contexts with their own language and ownership. Directed relationships identify upstream and downstream influence. Relationship labels name patterns such as customer-supplier, shared kernel, or anticorruption layer.\n\nBuild: Identify language boundaries through domain examples and ownership evidence. Name contexts by domain capability rather than repository technology. Document what each relationship exchanges and who controls changes. Cluster collaborating contexts while routing translation links outside node labels.\n\nCheck: A context is a model boundary, not automatically one microservice. Upstream direction denotes model influence rather than packet flow. Integration patterns reflect agreed behavior or are marked proposals. Shared boundaries have padded titles and no tangled relationship labels.\n\nPitfalls: Treating similarly named concepts as identical across contexts. Applying integration-pattern labels without agreement on their meaning.\n\nExample: Sales publishes an Order model; Fulfillment translates it into Shipment requests through an anticorruption layer.\n\nAlternatives: Domain aggregate diagram, System landscape diagram."
